review: W-026 (parallel workstreams: per-opus branching) — round 1
sella: qa-lead
verdict: FAIL

Checked against studio/briefs/W-026.md against packages/cli/src/branch.ts,
packages/cli/src/branch.test.ts, packages/cli/src/main.ts,
packages/commands/src/run.ts.

Blocking findings:

1. Brief behaviour 4 — "mergeOpusBranch refuses when opus state is not
   `done`" — is not implemented and not tested. mergeOpusBranch(repo, opusId)
   takes no opus/studio state at all; runMerge only checks that
   studio/opera/<id>.md *exists*, never its `state:` field. branch.test.ts's
   own "behaviour 4" tests something else entirely ("refuses missing
   branch"), so the 7-count in the dossier/handoff ("7-behaviour integration
   test suite") is reached by substituting an untested requirement with one
   nobody asked for. As shipped, `bisellium merge` will fast-forward any
   opus branch into master regardless of lifecycle state or gate status.
   Verified by reading branch.ts (no state read anywhere) and grepping the
   whole tree for a done/state check tied to merge — none exists.

2. Brief's own Interfaces section: "bisellium merge ... merge opus branch to
   master after gates pass, push, delete branch." mergeOpusBranch never
   pushes (grep for `push` in branch.ts: only Array.push, no git push), and
   never checks probationes gates (tests/lint/types) before merging. Only
   the branch-delete half of that sentence is implemented.

3. Behaviour 7 ("bisellium run --opus <id> forks worktree from opus branch,
   not HEAD") has zero test coverage anywhere in the repo. branch.test.ts
   doesn't touch run.ts; packages/shim/test/shim.test.ts's runCommand tests
   never pass --opus; grepped every *.test.ts for "--opus" — none exercises
   it. The wiring (packages/commands/src/run.ts:94-98,147-148, `base =
   opus/${opus}`) looks plausible by inspection only, unverified by any
   test.

4. Mutation test (qa lex §2: one mutation is enough to demonstrate the
   suite bites): inverted `canMerge.status !== 0` to `=== 0` in
   mergeOpusBranch's conflict-detection branch (the code path exercised by
   the suite's own "behaviour 5: refuses conflicting branch" test). All 10
   branch.test.ts checks still PASS. Root cause: both the "conflict" arm and
   the "clean but diverged" arm return `{ ok: false }` to the caller, and
   the test only asserts `result.ok === false`, never the specific error
   text — so the `git merge --no-commit --no-ff` conflict probe is
   currently provable dead code from the test's point of view. The suite
   does not actually bite on this behaviour.

Evidence checked and found sound (not re-litigated as findings):
- npm test green at tree 5ab03a0b415660335a02ec1a46197dfec027ee09 (verified
  independently: `sourceTreeHash` recomputed from HEAD 30fb51b matches; ran
  `node --import tsx packages/cli/src/branch.test.ts .` directly, 10/10 PASS).
- Behaviours 1, 2, 3, 6 (create/duplicate-refuse/ff-merge/branch-delete) are
  implemented and covered as claimed.
- tree/lint/types certificates are real (not a fabricated git object — the
  "tree:" id is @bisellium/shim's sourceTreeHash, a sha1 over `git ls-tree`
  excluding studio/.bisellium, by design not a git object id).

Verdict: FAIL. Send back to building: implement and test the `done`-state
(and gates-passed) guard on mergeOpusBranch, implement the push step, add a
real test for `run --opus`, and replace or supplement the conflict-detection
test so it can't pass with the check removed.
